\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55356 \u57252  FULL WORD-FOR-WORD PRESENTATION SCRIPT\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55358 \u56817  1. OPENING\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Hi, I\'92m Kyaw Min Thu.\uc0\u8232 I\'92m a controls engineer working on multi-domain industrial systems where control logic interacts directly with mechanical systems, gas flow, and physical processes.\
My main focus is diagnosing failures that are not obvious, especially when the issue crosses boundaries between control, mechanical, and process systems.\
Today I\'92ll walk through my experience briefly, and then focus on how I approach root cause analysis using real examples.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55358 \u56817  2. WHAT I DO\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 In many systems, when something fails, the first assumption is that the PLC or software is the problem.\
But in my experience, that\'92s often not the case.\
Most failures originate in the physical system\'97such as pressure, flow, or mechanical behavior\'97and the control system is simply responding to those conditions.\
So my approach is to first identify which domain is actually failing before making any changes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55358 \u56817  3. OFFSHORE EXPERIENCE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 I started working on offshore hydraulic systems, specifically top drive systems used in drilling operations.\
These systems involve multiple domains working together\'97hydraulic pressure, mechanical load, electrical control, and safety systems.\
In that environment, you cannot rely on trial and error. You must isolate the problem domain first\'97whether it\'92s hydraulic, mechanical, or control.\
This is where I learned that correct control logic does not guarantee correct system behavior.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55358 \u56817  4. TRANSITION TO RCA\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 That experience shaped how I approach troubleshooting.\
I don\'92t start by changing logic\'97I start by understanding system behavior and identifying which domain is responsible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55358 \u56817  5. RCA PHILOSOPHY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 My approach to root cause analysis is structured.\
I separate symptoms from root causes, and I validate each domain independently.\
I look at the control system, the electrical system, and the mechanical or process system separately.\
A system can behave correctly from the control perspective and still fail due to process conditions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55358 \u56817  6. RCA FRAMEWORK\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 I follow a consistent process.\
First, define the problem clearly.\uc0\u8232 Then isolate the domains.\uc0\u8232 Then collect real data.\uc0\u8232 Then validate the root cause.\uc0\u8232 Then implement a fix.\uc0\u8232 And finally, add prevention.\
The most important step here is data collection.\uc0\u8232 Without data, troubleshooting becomes guesswork.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55358 \u56817  7. CASE STUDY 1 \'97 FIRE SYSTEM\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 This system is a fire training simulator used for firefighter training.\uc0\u8232 It involves gas flow, air supply, and ignition control.\
The issue was intermittent ignition failure.\
Sometimes the fire would start immediately, and sometimes it required multiple attempts.\
From the control system perspective, everything appeared correct.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Investigation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 We verified the electrical signals.\uc0\u8232 The burner management system was triggering correctly.\uc0\u8232 The gas valves and air valves were opening properly.\
At this point, we eliminated control logic as the root cause.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Root Cause\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 The issue was insufficient air pressure during ignition.\
The air filter was clogged, which restricted airflow.\
The compressor would rebuild pressure over time, which made the failure appear random.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Key line (pause):\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Logic errors don\'92t self-recover. Process issues do.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Solution\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 We replaced the air filter.\
But more importantly, we added a pressure transmitter to monitor the air system.\
We also added SCADA monitoring and implemented a maintenance schedule.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Key takeaway:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Fixing the issue is not enough\'97you need to make the condition visible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55358 \u56817  8. CASE STUDY 2 \'97 INTERMITTENT SHUTDOWN\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 In this case, the system would shut down intermittently.\
It happened once a day or a few times per week.\
There was no logging system, and the issue could not be reproduced.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Strategy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 At that point, troubleshooting was not possible.\
So the first step was not debugging\'97it was creating visibility.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Key line:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 If you can\'92t see the event, you can\'92t solve it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Implementation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 I built a 24/7 monitoring system using Python.\
It captured all stopping conditions, permissives, interlocks, and command signals.\
It also sent real-time alerts to a Slack channel so we could respond immediately.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 Root Cause\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 We identified that a manual button was being intermittently triggered by an associate.\
This was not visible in standard PLC trending tools.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Key insight:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Standard tools were not enough, so I created a monitoring layer to capture real behavior.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55358 \u56817  9. COMPARISON\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 These two cases represent different types of failures.\
The fire system was a process issue solved by adding instrumentation.\
The shutdown issue was an intermittent problem solved by building a monitoring system.\
Different problems require different strategies\'97but both require real data.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55358 \u56817  10. RELEVANCE TO VELO3D\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 In systems like Velo3D, you have similar multi-domain interactions.\
Gas flow, thermal behavior, and motion control all interact with the control system.\
Failures may not originate in control logic\'97they can come from process conditions or physical interactions.\
The key is identifying which domain is responsible before making changes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u55358 \u56817  11. CLOSING\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 What I bring is strong root cause analysis capability, cross-domain system understanding, and a structured, data-driven approach.\
I focus on systems where control decisions directly impact physical outcomes, especially when failures are not obvious.\
And in those systems, structured analysis and real data make the difference.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
